                        "entry_id": "0432f3f4-5f44-34f1-97ca-20a4da5a903a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of or relating to a spasm; resembling a sudden contraction of the muscles."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "05587058-375a-3e8c-b3e6-1e87347a2817",
                        "entry_id": "0432f3f4-5f44-34f1-97ca-20a4da5a903a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Convulsive; consisting of spasms."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "7fe81def-cf61-42e8-b805-9945d4732a59",
                                "sense_id": "05587058-375a-3e8c-b3e6-1e87347a2817",
                                "text": "spasmodic asthma"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "832a3782-4436-43db-8a8e-e3f77667ab73",
                                "sense_id": "05587058-375a-3e8c-b3e6-1e87347a2817",
                                "text": "[…] Dr. Francis Fuller, […] upon wearing a Quick-ſilver Girdle, for the Cure of the Itch, (and that after an inconſiderate and raſh manner) was brought under a violent Spaſmodick Diſtemper, which was ſupposed by himſelf and others to be owing to ſome Mercurial Particles lodg'd in ſome excretory Ducts of the Brain."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a9885e85-dc7f-4ad2-b5c0-cb2a493268e8",
                                "sense_id": "05587058-375a-3e8c-b3e6-1e87347a2817",
                                "text": "Kennedy’s vocal rasp is not caused by an infection or respiratory condition. Instead, he has spasmodic dysphonia, a rare neurological condition in which the muscles that open and close his vocal cords spasm involuntarily, creating a strained or strangled quality to his speech."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4260bd36-254d-3d15-bd64-83141b4901a9",
                        "entry_id": "0432f3f4-5f44-34f1-97ca-20a4da5a903a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Intermittent or fitful; occurring in abrupt bursts."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e779ba97-eabb-47db-a0ce-f1ec262ca87a",
                                "sense_id": "4260bd36-254d-3d15-bd64-83141b4901a9",
                                "text": "spasmodic zeal or industry"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "49f3f47c-1abd-42eb-853b-f379d735bafa",
                                "sense_id": "4260bd36-254d-3d15-bd64-83141b4901a9",
                                "text": "The chin was heavy, the nostrils were low and wide, and the lower lip hung loosely except in his moments of spasmodic earnestness, when it shut like a steel trap."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0be2ef1c-3e3d-33af-b999-2ac6b662de69",
                        "entry_id": "0432f3f4-5f44-34f1-97ca-20a4da5a903a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Erratic or unsustained."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1ed3691a-9822-362f-9b17-acd9709d4b7e",
                        "entry_id": "0432f3f4-5f44-34f1-97ca-20a4da5a903a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of or relating to the spasmodic poets, a group of British Victorian poets who wrote introspective drama in verse."
